Q1. What is a Trigger in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

A Trigger is a stored PL/SQL program (database object) that
automatically executes when a specified event occurs on a table,
view or database.

Purpose

• Automate tasks.
• Enforce business rules.
• Maintain audit logs.
• Validate data.
• Synchronize related tables.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Triggers?

Interview Ready Answer

Triggers are used to automatically execute business logic whenever
a database event occurs without explicitly calling the trigger.

Advantages

• Automatic execution.
• Data integrity.
• Business rule enforcement.
• Audit logging.
• Reduces manual work.

Real-Life Example

Employee Audit System

Whenever a new employee is inserted into the Employee table,

↓

Automatically insert the employee details into the Employee_Audit
table.

======================================================================

Q3. What are the Types of Triggers in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

PL/SQL mainly provides four commonly used trigger types.

1. BEFORE Trigger
2. AFTER Trigger
3. Statement-Level Trigger
4. Row-Level Trigger

======================================================================

Q4. What is a BEFORE Trigger?

Interview Ready Answer

A BEFORE Trigger executes before the INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE
operation is performed.

Uses

• Validate data.
• Prevent invalid operations.
• Modify values before storing.

======================================================================

Q5. What is an AFTER Trigger?

Interview Ready Answer

An AFTER Trigger executes after the INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE
operation has completed successfully.

Uses

• Audit logging.
• Notifications.
• Maintain history tables.

======================================================================

Q6. What is a Statement-Level Trigger?

Interview Ready Answer

A Statement-Level Trigger executes once for an entire SQL statement,
regardless of the number of rows affected.

Example

UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = Salary + 1000;

If 100 rows are updated,

↓

Statement Trigger executes only once.

======================================================================

Q7. What is a Row-Level Trigger?

Interview Ready Answer

A Row-Level Trigger executes once for every row affected by an SQL
statement.

Example

UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = Salary + 1000;

If 100 rows are updated,

↓

Row-Level Trigger executes 100 times.

======================================================================

Q8. Difference between Statement-Level Trigger and Row-Level Trigger.

Interview Ready Answer

Statement-Level Trigger

• Executes once.
• Independent of affected rows.
• Faster for statement-based logic.

------------------------------------------------------------

Row-Level Trigger

• Executes once per affected row.
• Used for row validation.
• Suitable for row-specific business logic.

======================================================================

Q9. Difference between BEFORE Trigger and AFTER Trigger.

Interview Ready Answer

BEFORE Trigger

• Executes before DML operation.
• Used for validation.
• Can modify values before insert/update.

------------------------------------------------------------

AFTER Trigger

• Executes after successful DML operation.
• Used for auditing.
• Used for logging and notifications.

